WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.765
This table is a computer.

00:00:03.265 --> 00:00:10.370
No electricity, no chips: just plywood, brass sliders and a circular slide rule.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:16.239
It runs a neural network, the same arithmetic that underpins modern AI.

00:00:17.239 --> 00:00:18.911
Start with a handwritten digit,

00:00:19.311 --> 00:00:21.540
shrunk to thirty-six squares.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.823
Each square sets one slider on the outer ring:

00:00:26.323 --> 00:00:29.249
the more ink, the further it slides.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:37.043
The next ring holds the weights: numbers the network learned from thousands of handwritten digits.

00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:39.654
They are everything it knows.

00:00:40.654 --> 00:00:41.815
Now the work.

00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:43.244
Take an input,

00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:45.105
take its weight,

00:00:45.805 --> 00:00:48.267
and multiply them on the slide rule.

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:52.560
Add the answer to a slider in the hidden ring.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.692
Then do it again.

00:00:56.193 --> 00:00:58.515
Two hundred and sixteen times.

00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:04.687
Anything that ends up below zero goes back to zero.

00:01:06.087 --> 00:01:09.523
One more ring of weights, sixty more multiplications,

00:01:11.923 --> 00:01:15.081
and the ten inner sliders hold the answer.

00:01:15.881 --> 00:01:18.157
The one that has travelled furthest wins.

00:01:19.057 --> 00:01:20.171
It's a seven.

00:01:22.371 --> 00:01:24.647
Those weights make it read digits.

00:01:25.247 --> 00:01:27.011
Slide them to new positions,

00:01:29.011 --> 00:01:31.659
and the same table does a different job.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:37.024
Now each input slider answers a yes-or-no question about an animal.

00:01:37.624 --> 00:01:38.831
Hair: yes.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:40.810
Feathers: no.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.232
Milk: yes.

00:01:43.432 --> 00:01:46.172
The same two hundred and seventy-six steps,

00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:50.862
and it says mammal.

00:01:52.662 --> 00:01:53.962
Move the weights once more,

00:01:55.962 --> 00:01:58.052
and it guesses the next letter.

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:00.709
After T, H:

00:02:03.309 --> 00:02:04.006
E.

00:02:05.806 --> 00:02:08.778
By hand, each answer takes the best part of an hour.

00:02:09.778 --> 00:02:14.747
A chip in a data centre does the same sums trillions of times a second.

00:02:15.947 --> 00:02:18.223
But nothing different is happening in there.

00:02:18.723 --> 00:02:21.741
It is multiplying and adding, all the way down.

00:02:22.941 --> 00:02:25.217
Here, you can watch every step.
